==================================================
13) COMMENTARY AS TO WHY (RATIONALE)
==================================================
1) Header and Time/Date Enhancements:
- Why: I wanted users to have immediate awareness of time while planning deadlines.
- Outcome: The header now feels more functional and balanced, and users can check time/date without leaving the page.

2) Dark Mode and Shared Nav Cleanup:
- Why: I had duplicate theme scripts across pages, which caused conflicts and inconsistent rendering.
- Outcome: By centralizing this logic, behavior is more reliable and future updates are easier to manage.

3) Profile Icon Upload/Persist/Remove:
- Why: I wanted profile personalization, but also needed a clear undo path if the wrong image was chosen.
- Outcome: Users can upload, keep it across pages/refresh, and remove/reset quickly when needed.

4) Toast Consolidation:
- Why: I had repeated notification blocks in multiple files, which was hard to maintain.
- Outcome: One shared toast utility now keeps feedback consistent and reduces repeated code.

5) Notes Rendering/Typography Consistency:
- Why: Note spacing and readability were inconsistent between pages and sometimes looked cramped.
- Outcome: Notes are easier to scan, preserve user-entered formatting better, and now look consistent across views.

6) About Page Fixes:
- Why: I identified path and structure issues that could break expected behavior.
- Outcome: The About page is now cleaner structurally and more reliable.

7) Dashboard Module Filter Auto-Learning:
- Why: Hardcoded modules did not scale once users started adding custom modules.
- Outcome: The filter now reflects real assignment data automatically, with less manual upkeep.

8) Add Assignment Module Suggestions:
- Why: Re-entering module names repeatedly creates friction and inconsistent naming.
- Outcome: Suggestions speed up entry and help keep module naming more consistent.

9) Dashboard Reset Filters + Dark Mode Adherence:
- Why: Users could get stuck in narrow filter states, and one control had inconsistent dark-mode styling.
- Outcome: Reset Filters restores the full list quickly and the control now visually matches both themes.

10) Optional Button Sounds:
- Why: I wanted feedback when users save/update/delete without forcing audio on everyone.
- Outcome: Sound feedback is available, and users can keep it enabled or muted through the shared preference setting.

11) Export/Import Clarification:
- Why: There was uncertainty about whether export should open a picker.
- Outcome: The behavior is now clear: export downloads directly, while import uses a file picker.

12) Asset Strategy:
- Why: Keeping shared behavior in one place reduces repetition and update complexity.
- Outcome: The codebase is easier to maintain and less likely to drift between pages.

13) End-State Stability:
- Why: After multiple iterative changes, I needed to confirm everything still worked as one system.
- Outcome: The current build is stable, cohesive, and safer to extend.

==================================================
14) UNIVERSITY STUDENT COMMENTARY (EVIDENCE-BASED REFLECTION)
==================================================
As the developer, I did not approach these edits as isolated visual tweaks. I treated them as iterative improvements against core software quality themes: usability, consistency, maintainability, and robustness.

1) Usability and User Experience:
- The live header time/date was introduced because users repeatedly referenced schedule awareness while managing deadlines.
- The final styling decisions (spacing, fade treatment, darker date contrast, bold/italic date) were based on readability in both light and dark visual contexts.
- Adding Reset Filters and module suggestions reduced interaction cost: users can recover quickly from narrow filter states and avoid repetitive module typing.

2) Consistency Across Pages:
- A recurring problem was behavioral drift between pages (dashboard/add/overview/about), especially for notes and theme handling.
- Mirroring note rendering and typography between dashboard and overview was essential to preserve trust in the same data shown in different views.
- Shared scripts for navigation/theme/notifications/time reduced page-by-page divergence.

3) Maintainability and Technical Debt Reduction:
- Consolidating duplicated logic into shared assets directly reduced technical debt.
- Before consolidation, the same behavior existed in multiple files, which increased the probability of introducing regressions when changing one page and not another.
- After consolidation, updates are cheaper and safer because shared behavior has a single update path.

4) Reliability and Fault Tolerance:
- Profile image upload was implemented with persistence and rollback in mind: localStorage saves user state, and Remove photo provides a non-destructive recovery path.
- Fallback image handling was necessary because path variability and missing assets can break UX.
- Export/import behavior was clarified and verified so user expectations match actual browser behavior.

5) Data Quality and Information Architecture:
- Dynamic module learning in filters and suggestions improves data quality by encouraging reuse of existing module names.
- This reduces fragmented values caused by manual re-entry and strengthens filtering accuracy.

6) Verification Approach:
- Changes were validated with repeated error checks and behavior checks after edits.
- This process helped catch integration issues (for example, selector placement affecting dark-mode button styling) and resolve them before finalizing.

7) Trade-offs and Constraints:
- localStorage-based profile persistence was selected for simplicity and no-backend constraints, but has storage limits for very large images.
- Inline style usage in some generated markup was retained where it provided quick cross-page consistency, though further CSS extraction would improve long-term clarity.

8) Academic Reflection:
- The key lesson is that UI polish is most valuable when paired with structural refactoring.
- The project improved not only in appearance but in code organization and predictability.
- Future work should continue this pattern: move remaining inline styles to shared CSS, add small automated regression checks, and further standardize component behavior.

Conclusion:
These changes are backed by practical software engineering outcomes: reduced duplication, improved consistency, stronger resilience, and lower interaction friction for end users. The result is a cleaner and more maintainable submission aligned with university expectations for both implementation quality and reflective development practice.

==================================================
15) ADDITIONAL STUDENT CRITICAL COMMENTARY
==================================================
1) What I would improve next (critical self-evaluation):
- While functionality is improved, some inline styles still exist and should be migrated into shared CSS modules for cleaner separation of concerns.
- The project currently relies on manual verification; adding lightweight automated checks (for key UI logic and storage behavior) would increase confidence during future edits.
- A dedicated configuration layer for repeated constants (colors, durations, labels) would further reduce drift and improve portability.

2) Alternatives considered and why they were not selected:
- Backend profile image storage was considered but not chosen due to project scope and no-server constraints.
- Framework migration (component-based frontend) was considered but deferred because it would create high refactor cost for limited short-term value in this assignment stage.
- Keeping duplicated page logic was rejected because short-term convenience would worsen long-term maintenance and bug risk.

3) Accessibility and inclusion perspective:
- The visual updates improved contrast and spacing for readability, but formal accessibility testing (keyboard-only flow, ARIA audit, contrast tooling) should be completed in the next iteration.
- Profile image and notification interactions are user-friendly, but explicit status announcements for assistive technologies could still be improved.

4) Risk management reflection:
- Centralizing shared logic reduced structural risk, but also increased dependency on shared files.
- To manage this, changes were validated after each edit and fallback paths were added (for example, image fallback and safe notification checks).

5) Professional development reflection:
- The most important learning was to pair visible UI improvements with architectural cleanup.
- This approach produced stronger outcomes than visual changes alone, because it improved both user experience and development sustainability.
- Iterative feedback cycles were highly effective: small, reversible changes made it easier to converge on acceptable UX quickly.

Final reflective statement:
From a student engineering perspective, the project now demonstrates not only feature delivery but disciplined improvement of code quality, user-centered interaction design, and reflective practice. The next milestone should focus on accessibility verification and small automated regression checks to support continued iteration with lower risk.

